 - `service_uuid` / `notify_uuid` — WT901BLE GATT UUIDs (defaults match the common nRF52-based WT901BLE modules)
 - `frame_id` — IMU frame for the published message header
 - `topic` — topic the `sensor_msgs/Imu` message is published on
-- `scan_timeout` / `reconnect_wait_seconds` — BLE scan/reconnect tuning
+- `scan_timeout` — upper bound on a scan; the scan stops as soon as the device
+  is matched, so this is only paid when the device is absent
+- `reconnect_wait_seconds` — wait before starting a fresh scan after a failure
+- `connect_attempts` / `connect_retry_wait_seconds` — how many times to retry
+  `connect()` in place before falling back to a full rescan. BlueZ routinely
+  aborts the first connect right after discovery stops, and retrying in place
+  is far cheaper than another scan window.
 
 ## Notes
 - Orientation is derived from the device's onboard roll/pitch/yaw fusion
@@ -57,5 +63,18 @@ ros2 run witmotion_ros2 witmotion_ros2 --ros-args \
 - If your specific WT901BLE variant uses different service/notify UUIDs,
   override them in `config/witmotion.yaml`.
 
+## Shutdown
+The node disconnects the peripheral on exit. This matters: if the process dies
+with the GATT link still open, BlueZ keeps the device marked connected, the
+sensor stops advertising, and the *next* run cannot find it by scanning at all.
+Give the process enough time to stop on its own — under `ros2 launch`, that
+means a `sigterm_timeout` larger than the launch default of 5 seconds. If a link
+does get stranded, clear it with:
+
+```sh
+bluetoothctl info <MAC>        # Connected: yes means the link was stranded
+bluetoothctl disconnect <MAC>
+```
